  <p class="note-line">
    Ranges are directional, based on current admissions information and institute data at the time of
    writing. Verify current entrance exam requirements, fees, and placement records against the specific
    institute before enrolling.
  </p>

  <p>
    Neither route requires NATA, and neither carries architecture's mandatory 100-day, Council-regulated
    licensing internship. That is a genuine advantage for anyone who wants to start earning sooner, but it
    also means the entrance filter, and therefore the peer group and placement support, varies far more
    wildly between interior design institutes than it does between COA-recognised architecture colleges.
  </p>

  <h2 id="no-licence">The no-licensing-body reality nobody explains upfront</h2>

  <p>
    This is the single biggest structural difference between interior design and architecture, and it
    rarely comes up in career counselling conversations, even though it changes almost everything about
    how the career actually works.
  </p>

  <p>
    There is no Indian law equivalent to the Architects Act, 1972 for interior designers. Nobody needs a
    government-recognised licence to use the title "interior designer," sign a design plan, or run a
    studio. The Indian Institute of Interior Designers (IIID) is the field's main professional body, and it
    offers voluntary membership across Associate, Member, and Fellow grades, typically Rs 5,000-10,000 a
    year, but it confers no legal licence and is not required to work.
  </p>

  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--amber">
    <p>
      This cuts both ways. It means almost anyone with a diploma, or even without one, can legally start
      taking paying clients tomorrow, which is exactly why the field feels so accessible compared with
      architecture or engineering. It also means a client has no legal guarantee of quality or accountability
      when they hire an interior designer, which is precisely why building visible proof of work, real
      client references, and a specific specialisation matters more here than in a licensed profession
      where the credential itself does some of that trust-building automatically.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p>
    In practice, this means the market sorts designers by portfolio, referrals, and specialisation, not by
    a registration number. A genuinely strong designer with no IIID membership can out-earn a mediocre one
    with full credentials, in a way that simply does not happen in architecture. That freedom is real
    opportunity for someone willing to build proof deliberately, and a real risk for someone who assumes
    the diploma alone will do the selling.
  </p>

