  <h2 id="ai-risk">What AI is actually changing for interior designers</h2>

  <p>
    Two opposite reassurances get repeated to worried students: "AI can never replace human taste" and
    "AI is about to wipe out interior design jobs." Look at what is actually moving task by task, and
    both of those turn out to be the wrong question.
  </p>

  <p>
    AI rendering platforms such as Foyr Neo, AiHouse, Spacely AI, and ReRoom can already turn a SketchUp,
    Revit, or Blender model into a photorealistic render in seconds, and some industry risk assessments
    place interior designers among the roles most exposed to image-generating AI, in the same category as
    architects and art directors. Basic AI-generated mood boards and layout suggestions are already
    available cheaply or free online, a genuine race to the bottom on pricing for pure visualisation work.
  </p>

  <div class="comparison-grid">
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--gold">
      <div class="compare-card__label">What is shrinking</div>
      <ul>
        <li>Manual, hand-produced renders and mood boards with no design ownership behind them.</li>
        <li>First-pass layout and colour-scheme suggestions done entirely without an AI tool.</li>
        <li>Generic "here are three style options" consultations with no budget or vendor grounding.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--teal">
      <div class="compare-card__label">What is growing</div>
      <ul>
        <li>The judgment to catch an AI-generated layout that ignores a real electrical point, load-bearing wall, or fire-exit rule.</li>
        <li>Client consultation and the ability to pivot fast when a client changes their mind midway through a project.</li>
        <li>Vendor negotiation, site coordination, and delivery ownership that a rendering tool cannot perform.</li>
        <li>Specialised commercial and hospitality design judgment, where code compliance and brand consistency matter as much as aesthetics.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p>
    The practical takeaway for someone still deciding on this field: AI is making designers faster at the
    routine visualisation parts of the job, not making client judgment unnecessary. There is a genuine risk
    worth naming honestly too: if new designers lean on AI renders without ever learning to judge whether a
    suggestion actually respects a client's budget and a building's real constraints, they end up with a
    fast portfolio and weak judgment, exactly the gap experienced designers get hired to fix. The staged,
    realistic path is to use AI rendering tools now to compress the slow visualisation parts of the job,
    then, as adoption matures over the next few years, spend the freed-up time on the specialisation and
    client-relationship work that AI genuinely cannot replace.
  </p>

  <h2 id="vs-architecture">Interior design vs architecture: the honest trade-off</h2>

  <p>
    This comparison drives a large share of the confusion behind this exact keyword, so it deserves a
    direct answer instead of a diplomatic dodge.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>What actually differs</th>
          <th>Interior design</th>
          <th>Architecture</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="What actually differs"><strong>Training duration</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Interior design">6 months to 2 years (diploma) or 4 years (B.Des), with no mandatory licensing internship.</td>
          <td data-label="Architecture">5 years (B.Arch), including a mandatory minimum 100-day training internship inside the degree.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="What actually differs"><strong>Legal licensing to practise</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Interior design">None. IIID membership is voluntary and adds credibility but is not legally required.</td>
          <td data-label="Architecture">Council of Architecture registration under the Architects Act, 1972 is a legal requirement to use the title or sign drawings.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="What actually differs"><strong>Entry barrier</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Interior design">Low; most diploma institutes require no national entrance exam.</td>
          <td data-label="Architecture">Higher; requires NATA or JEE Paper 2, plus COA-recognised college verification.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="What actually differs"><strong>What you can legally own</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Interior design">Space planning, furnishing, materials, and non-structural fitout work; cannot sign off on structural changes.</td>
          <td data-label="Architecture">Full structural design authorship and legal sign-off responsibility for a whole building.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="What actually differs"><strong>Long-term income ceiling</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Interior design">Wide upside through independent practice or hospitality/commercial specialisation, reachable faster since no licensing runway is required.</td>
          <td data-label="Architecture">Wide upside through independent practice, typically reached over a longer 7-10 year runway due to the licensing and training structure.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>
    Neither field is objectively better. The honest deciding question is not "which one sounds more
    prestigious," because architecture usually wins that comparison by default. It is: do you want
    authorship over a building's structure and legal responsibility for it, or does space, materiality, and
    client-facing styling interest you more, with a faster, cheaper path to your first paid client? Answer
    that question honestly before family or social pressure answers it for you.
  </p>

