        <li>A roughly $35 billion Indian market in 2026, growing at close to 13% a year, with residential demand alone compounding faster still.</li>
        <li>A realistic income timeline: a modest first role or early freelance income, steadier growth by year three with a chosen specialisation, and a wider ceiling once a referral base or independent studio forms.</li>
        <li>One visible proof step already taken, like a real small project, a cleaned-up portfolio, or a shortlisted application to a turnkey platform, not just an intention to "study interiors."</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p>
    A question that comes up in almost every family discussion and rarely gets a straight answer: is this
    a realistic, safe career for a daughter, given that some relatives picture endless site visits and
    late-evening client calls. In practice, interior design skews heavily toward desk, showroom, and
    client-meeting work, not construction-site labour, and women make up a large share of designers, studio
    founders, and design leads at platforms like Livspace and HomeLane as well as independent practices
    across Indian cities. Site visits do happen, but they are scheduled in advance, not a daily requirement,
    and the physical demands are nowhere close to a site-heavy construction or civil-engineering role. If
    safety or travel is the real worry, the useful step is asking a specific studio or platform what their
    actual site-visit pattern looks like, not ruling out the whole profession on assumption.
  </p>

  <h2 id="next-step">What to do next</h2>

  <p>
    Another week of turning "is interior design a good career" over in your head, in the abstract, will not
    settle anything. Neither will letting a relative's opinion about "creative, tasteful fields" make the
    call on your behalf.
  </p>

  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--teal">
    <p>
      Run yourself through The 4-Checkpoint Protocol above, honestly, on paper, for the actual institute and
      city you are considering.
    </p>
    <p>
      Then pass The 3 Gates on one small real design project or a modular-platform application before you
      commit course fees and time to a specific diploma or degree.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p>
    Achieving earlier financial freedom through interior design comes down to building a genuine
    <strong>high-value skill portfolio</strong> on top of the training, real design proof, a chosen
    specialisation, and the ability to explain and sell your decisions clearly, not the word "interior
    designer" on a business card alone. Move toward that with
